[Chapter PDF](print/chapter-04.pdf) · [Session 5](seminar/index.html#week-05)

A debugger can stop between the two assignments of a sequence. At the stopping point there is a current state and a remaining command. Together these form a *configuration*. A transition system describes which configuration may come next. It can describe a computation even when there is no final state.

*Sources.* For one-step executions see Nipkow and Klein, _Concrete Semantics_, §7.3. [Book PDF.](https://concrete-semantics.org/concrete-semantics.pdf) John C. Reynolds, _Theories of Programming Languages_, §6.2 on inference rules for transitions. Our particular choice of atomic steps follows the reduction relation `red` in the Lean port of Xavier Leroy’s compiler verification course. In both of our IMP semantics arithmetic expressions and tests are evaluated in full when their results are needed. The shared task is to construct an infinite sequence with every adjacent pair justified by a transition rule. [Reynolds, chapter 6.](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~jcr/tpl-toc.html) [Leroy, Imp, reduction semantics.](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean)

# The step relation
%%%
tag := "ch04-s1"
%%%

Write $`\left\langle {c,s} \right\rangle\rightarrow\left\langle {d,t} \right\rangle` for one step from command $`c` and store $`s` to residual command $`d` and store $`t`. A residual command records the work still to do. The syntax and state operations are those of Chapter 2. As before $`B\left( {b,s} \right)` means the test is true. Use $`I` for `if b then c else d` and $`W` for `while b do c`.

*Definition 5.1.* The step relation is generated by the following rules. Skip has no outgoing rule.

$$`\begin{matrix} \left\langle {x \mathrel{:=} e,s} \right\rangle & {\rightarrow\left\langle {\operatorname{skip},s\left\lbrack {x\mapsto\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,s} \right)} \right\rbrack} \right\rangle} \\ \left\langle {\operatorname{skip}\text{;}\ d,s} \right\rangle & {\rightarrow\left\langle {d,s} \right\rangle} \end{matrix}`

$$`\frac{\left\langle {c,s} \right\rangle\rightarrow\left\langle {c',t} \right\rangle}{\left\langle {c\ \text{;}\ d,s} \right\rangle\rightarrow\left\langle {c'\ \text{;}\ d,t} \right\rangle}`

$$`\begin{matrix} {B\left( {b,s} \right)} & {\Longrightarrow\left\langle {I,s} \right\rangle\rightarrow\left\langle {c,s} \right\rangle} \\ {\neg B\left( {b,s} \right)} & {\Longrightarrow\left\langle {I,s} \right\rangle\rightarrow\left\langle {d,s} \right\rangle} \\ {B\left( {b,s} \right)} & {\Longrightarrow\left\langle {W,s} \right\rangle\rightarrow\left\langle {c\ \text{;}\ W,s} \right\rangle} \\ {\neg B\left( {b,s} \right)} & {\Longrightarrow\left\langle {W,s} \right\rangle\rightarrow\left\langle {\operatorname{skip},s} \right\rangle} \end{matrix}`

The third rule lifts a step of the left operand into a sequence. There is no rule taking an arbitrary step of the right operand while the left operand is pending. The true loop rule installs a fresh copy of the body before the next guard test. Some textbooks instead unfold the loop to a conditional in one unconditional step. The choice changes trace lengths, so a proof that counts steps must use one convention consistently.

The syntax of a residual command determines where to search for its next instruction. For `(a; b); d`, inspect the left child `a; b`. If `a` can step, its step is first lifted through the inner sequence and then through the outer one. These two applications of the lifting rule derive a *single* transition of the whole command. If `a` is skip, the inner sequence may instead discard it. The enclosing sequence lifts that transition once. In neither case is the rightmost command inspected. The number of rule applications used to *justify* a step can therefore exceed one even when the number of executed steps is exactly one.

This decomposition also establishes uniqueness of the successor for the given grammar. Induct on the command. Assignment has exactly the prescribed result. In a conditional or loop the Boolean guard determines a single rule. In a sequence with left skip, lifting would require an impossible step from skip. In a sequence with any other left command, discarding is impossible and the induction hypothesis identifies the lifted successor when one exists. Together with progress this gives one successor at every nonterminal configuration, a fact that will help relate negative and positive accounts of divergence.

# Finite paths and termination
%%%
tag := "ch04-s2"
%%%

For configurations $`p,q`, write $`p\rightarrow^{n}q` when there is a list of $`n + 1` configurations beginning at $`p`, ending at $`q`, and related by a step at each adjacent pair. The natural number $`n` counts edges. Write $`p\rightarrow^{*}q` if such an $`n` exists. Length zero gives reflexivity. Concatenation adds path lengths and gives transitivity. Termination from $`\left\langle {c,s} \right\rangle` with result $`t` means $`\left\langle {c,s} \right\rangle\rightarrow^{*}\left\langle {\operatorname{skip},t} \right\rangle`.

*Example 5.2.* Let $`a` abbreviate `x := 2`, $`d` abbreviate `y := x`, with $`x \neq y`, and put $`u = s\left\lbrack {x\mapsto 2} \right\rbrack`, $`t = u\left\lbrack {y\mapsto 2} \right\rbrack`. The complete path is

$$`\left\langle {a\ \text{;}\ d,s} \right\rangle\rightarrow\left\langle {\operatorname{skip}\text{;}\ d,u} \right\rangle\rightarrow\left\langle {d,u} \right\rangle\rightarrow\left\langle {\operatorname{skip},t} \right\rangle.`

The middle step changes the remaining syntax and leaves the store unchanged. A finite prefix that stops at $`\left\langle {d,u} \right\rangle` has yet to execute the second assignment.

*Lemma 5.3 (Progress of commands).* For every command and state, either the command is skip or the configuration can step. Prove this by structural induction. An assignment can step by the first rule. For a conditional or a loop, totality of the Boolean test selects a rule. In a sequence, apply the induction hypothesis to the left operand in the given state. If it is skip, discard it. Otherwise lift the supplied step. This completes the cases.

An irreducible configuration has no successor. An irreducible configuration with a residual command other than skip would be *stuck*. The lemma excludes stuck states for this grammar. Extending the language with a partial expression such as integer division would require a decision about division by zero and a new progress argument.

There are two different closures in common use. Allowing zero steps means that skip terminates from every state even though it has no successor. Requiring at least one step would omit precisely such zero-length runs. A nonempty path may also return to its starting point if the transition system has a cycle. Therefore testing whether endpoints differ cannot decide which closure a given piece of evidence inhabits. One must retain the length or at least a first edge.

For a concrete use of concatenation, suppose a path takes a conditional with a true guard to its then branch in one step, and a second path evaluates that branch to skip in three steps. Their join is valid only when the first store and residual at the cut match the starting store and residual of the second path. Removing one of the two copies of the cut configuration leaves five configurations and four edges. Cutting the resulting path after its second edge is equally legitimate, but the new cut point might fall *inside* an assignment sequence. Path decomposition is determined by a chosen position; decomposing a big-step derivation instead follows its last rule.

# Infinite paths
%%%
tag := "ch04-s3"
%%%

An infinite execution from $`p` is a function $`q` from natural numbers to configurations with $`q(0) = p` and $`q(i)\rightarrow q\left( {i + 1} \right)` for every $`i`. To exhibit divergence it suffices to construct such a function. For $`W =` `while true do skip`, fix any state $`s`. Put $`A = \left\langle {W,s} \right\rangle` and $`C = \left\langle {\operatorname{skip}\text{;}\ W,s} \right\rangle`. The true loop rule gives $`A\rightarrow C`. The rule discarding skip gives $`C\rightarrow A`. Set $`q\left( {2i} \right) = A` and $`q\left( {2i + 1} \right) = C`. Every natural index has one of these forms, so the two displayed steps verify all the required edges.

A run can also be infinite without repeating a configuration. In `while true do x := x + 1`, each return to the loop head from an initial state with $`x = 0` has a larger value of $`x`. Divergence means the availability of a successor along an entire infinite execution. Revisiting an identical configuration is a sufficient witness here, and is unnecessary in the definition.

For deterministic IMP we can strengthen the connection between the two observations. Suppose there is no finite path from a given configuration to skip. That configuration cannot already contain skip, since a path of length zero would suffice. Progress supplies its next configuration. A finite path to skip from the successor would extend backwards across the chosen edge to give one from the initial configuration. Hence the same absence property holds at the successor. Iterating this argument defines the configuration at each natural index. Uniqueness of the successor makes the resulting infinite sequence independent of any selection among alternatives.

In the other direction assume an infinite sequence and a finite path to skip start at the same configuration. An induction on the finite length shows that both executions agree at every position up to its endpoint: the starts agree, and determinacy identifies their next configurations. The infinite sequence would consequently have to take a step from skip at the next index, contrary to the rules. This particular exclusion uses determinacy. In a nondeterministic language, the existence of an infinite execution can coexist with a different, terminating execution from the same input; Chapter 35 will exploit exactly that difference.

An infinite path requires a successor even at its first index. This short theorem rules out such a path from *any* irreducible configuration `p`, whether successful or stuck. Lean's `Nat → C` is the type of a sequence of configurations, and `step` supplies its adjacent-edge condition.

```lean
example {C : Type} (step : C → C → Prop) (p : C)
    (terminal : ∀ q, ¬ step p q) :
    ¬ ∃ path : Nat → C, path 0 = p ∧ ∀ n, step (path n) (path (n+1)) := by
  rintro ⟨path, start, edges⟩
  have first := edges 0
  rw [start] at first
  exact terminal _ first
```

# Exercises
%%%
tag := "ch04-s4"
number := false
%%%

*5.1.* How many steps does `if true then skip else x := 1` take to terminate? Compare with skip using our definition of termination.

*5.2.* Show that a positive-length path starting at $`\left\langle {\operatorname{skip},s} \right\rangle` is impossible. Decide whether the star relation nevertheless relates that configuration to itself.

*5.3.* For the always-incrementing loop, write its configuration at times $`3i`, $`3i + 1`, and $`3i + 2` to construct an infinite execution.

*Further problem.* Extend the command syntax with `abort`, a command with no outgoing step, keeping skip as the sole successful result. Starting from `(x := 1); abort`, exhibit a maximal finite path. Which lemma in this chapter fails? Can one conclude that an execution is infinite merely because it cannot reach skip?

