*Definitions · 10 minutes.* Mark the command, store, and one-step arrow on the three-step sequence in §5.2.

*Joint proof · 30 minutes.* Construct the infinite execution in §5.3. Have participants state the domain of the witness function and justify every even and odd edge.

*Proof review · 20 minutes.* Try the construction on the incrementing loop. Use progress to explain why a stuck term and an infinite run would represent distinct phenomena in an extended language.

*Facilitator’s hint.* After one true loop step the residual command is a sequence. Consult the leftmost command before choosing the next rule.

# Solutions and Further Reading
%%%
tag := "ch04-s6"
number := false
%%%

*5.1.* The conditional takes one step to $`\left\langle {\operatorname{skip},s} \right\rangle`, while skip already terminates by a zero-length path. Both have final state $`s`.

*5.2.* A positive-length path has a first edge. No conclusion of Definition 5.1 starts with skip. The zero-edge path still proves reflexivity.

*5.3.* Define $`s_{i}` by $`s_{i}(x) = i` and let it agree with the initial state elsewhere. With body $`a` and loop $`W`, the three configurations are $`\left\langle {W,s_{i}} \right\rangle`, $`\left\langle {a\ \text{;}\ W,s_{i}} \right\rangle`, and $`\left\langle {\operatorname{skip}\text{;}\ W,s_{i + 1}} \right\rangle`. Discarding skip returns to $`\left\langle {W,s_{i + 1}} \right\rangle`. These edges justify the witness at every index.

For the source definitions compare `star` (finite reflexive transitive closure) and `infseq` (an infinite sequence of steps). The proof here gives an explicit periodic witness. [Leroy, Sequences.](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Sequences.lean)

*Further problem.* At an arbitrary initial store `s`, the first edge lifts the assignment step and reaches `⟨skip; abort, s[x ↦ 1]⟩`. The next edge discards the leading skip, reaching `⟨abort, s[x ↦ 1]⟩`. This path is maximal, meaning that no further edge can extend it. In particular its last store is well defined, but the residual command is different from the designated successful final command. Recording only the last store of any maximal finite path would mistake this outcome for successful return.

The progress lemma fails already at `⟨abort,s⟩`, which is neither at skip nor able to step. There is no infinite execution from the displayed starting configuration: the first two edges are forced and a third is impossible. Thus failure to reach skip is compatible with a finite stuck execution in the extended transition system. One alternative design makes abort a designated exceptional outcome. That design would require separate definitions of successful and exceptional termination and a corresponding reformulation of progress.
